1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,000
And it has cleared the tower.

2
00:00:02,001 --> 00:00:10,000
As they roar into the sky, NASA's space shuttles are the perfect symbols of incredible human vision and mastery of technology.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
But like all machines, they sometimes go very wrong.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Billions of dollars and years of hard work have vanished in a few tragic moments.

5
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Obviously a major malfunction.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:32,000
More important, 14 people have paid with their lives for shuttle disasters that should have been avoided.

7
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:36,000
We now know the danger is not only in the machines.

8
00:00:36,001 --> 00:00:40,000
The invitation to disaster is also in ourselves.

9
00:00:55,171 --> 00:01:00,700
The history of the machine frontier is it attracts people who are prone to bravado,

10
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:03,880
great confidence, strong personalities.

11
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:08,340
But we know now that that has to be held in check.

12
00:01:08,341 --> 00:01:18,760
Instead, that has to be tempered with respect, with humility, and with concern for the workers.

13
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:26,760
I'm going to step off the limb now.

14
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:35,080
That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.

15
00:01:35,081 --> 00:01:41,320
Even as Neil Armstrong and the others who followed him were walking on the lunar surface,

16
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:47,560
the NASA program that had taken them on their amazing journeys was undergoing wrenching changes.

17
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:56,140
The budget for NASA's hugely successful Apollo program was being slashed.

18
00:01:56,860 --> 00:02:02,400
By the early 1970s, NASA desperately needed something new to regain support.

19
00:02:02,401 --> 00:02:10,340
In the game of money, power, and prestige that is played in Washington politics, NASA's seat at the table was now on the line.

20
00:02:12,100 --> 00:02:16,420
The Apollo program, a mighty achievement, is now winding down.

21
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:20,890
NASA looks inside itself and says, what do we do now?

22
00:02:20,891 --> 00:02:23,420
How do we follow and act like that?

23
00:02:23,421 --> 00:02:29,780
We had all those engineers, administrators, great prestige, great pride in what they had done.

24
00:02:29,781 --> 00:02:33,500
we needed something else to do in space.

25
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:35,480
The space shuttle was the answer.

26
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:43,960
NASA engineers came up with a concept that they believed would assure the continuation of manned spaceflight for decades.

27
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:48,080
It was known officially as the Space Transportation System.

28
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:51,340
The public would soon know it as the Space Shuttle.

29
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:58,400
The shuttle concept was sold as a reusable craft that would allow the nation to continue space exploration.

30
00:02:58,401 --> 00:03:02,560
and it would do it without spending the billions of dollars on rockets

31
00:03:02,561 --> 00:03:06,960
that had always been destroyed, falling to Earth after spacecraft reached orbit.

32
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:10,260
And best of all, the shuttle would pay for itself.

33
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:15,840
Apollo was pure R&D, but the shuttle was put in the position

34
00:03:15,841 --> 00:03:18,340
from the beginning of needing to pay its own way.

35
00:03:18,341 --> 00:03:22,340
If they launched a certain number of missions a year and carried payloads,

36
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,740
and the word payload is very significant here,

37
00:03:25,741 --> 00:03:32,060
They got money from the Department of Defense, from scientific institutes for carrying experiments.

38
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:37,900
The planners were so optimistic about the shuttle, they were confident a mission could be launched each week or two.

39
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:42,780
Congress approved the budget, and the first of four shuttles went into production.

40
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:49,380
The shuttle that was sold to Congress was to be a huge integrated system.

41
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:54,880
The orbiter itself was a stubby-winged aircraft about the size of a commercial DC-9.

42
00:03:54,881 --> 00:04:00,280
It would be bolted to a mammoth fuel tank filled with liquid hydrogen and oxygen.

43
00:04:00,281 --> 00:04:05,400
The orbiter was to be wedged between two solid rocket boosters that would top off

44
00:04:05,401 --> 00:04:10,960
its power at seven million pounds of thrust. Once lit, this incredible

45
00:04:10,961 --> 00:04:15,940
propulsion unit would burn 2.2 million pounds of fuel in just two minutes.

46
00:04:15,941 --> 00:04:20,480
After they were used up, the solid fuel boosters would be jettisoned for reuse.

47
00:04:20,481 --> 00:04:23,560
only the giant liquid fuel tank would be disposable.

48
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:32,320
The space shuttle is arguably the most complex machine ever built.

49
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:40,740
Two and a half million parts must combine to hurtle a craft weighing 2,000 tons into orbit,

50
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:42,500
hundreds of miles above the Earth.

51
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:49,840
After the mission, the entire magnificent machine must survive a return to Earth

52
00:04:49,841 --> 00:04:54,420
that would require it to go from 18,000 miles per hour to zero

53
00:04:54,421 --> 00:04:57,160
in the time it takes to wash a load of clothes.

54
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:06,500
The first shuttle to take off was the Columbia in April of 1981.

55
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:11,360
But as the headlines subsided and the public began to take the shuttle for granted,

56
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:12,880
problems appeared.

57
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:17,020
It was clear almost from the start that the shuttle could never keep

58
00:05:17,021 --> 00:05:20,200
the ambitious number of flights per year NASA had promised.

59
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:27,880
By 1985, launch facilities NASA intended to be busy every week were sitting idle.

60
00:05:28,940 --> 00:05:32,240
Managers became frantic to speed up the lagging number of launches.

61
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:37,300
Sixty missions had originally been predicted for 1985.

62
00:05:37,301 --> 00:05:40,440
The number to actually lift off was only nine.

63
00:05:40,441 --> 00:05:49,260
NASA hoped, promised, wrote papers to the effect, that in time the shuttle would become

64
00:05:49,261 --> 00:05:54,940
as reliable and as frequent a flyer almost as an airliner.

65
00:05:54,941 --> 00:06:01,200
But it was clear to people on the inside that this was a whole different animal.

66
00:06:01,201 --> 00:06:03,520
The shuttle program was in trouble.

67
00:06:03,521 --> 00:06:09,160
Its budgets continued to be cut by Congress while its costs were increasing.

68
00:06:09,161 --> 00:06:13,560
The original sales pitch for the shuttle, as I remember it,

69
00:06:13,561 --> 00:06:20,960
promised payloads in orbit for around $100 a pound cost.

70
00:06:20,961 --> 00:06:27,220
The actual cost has always been around $10,000 a pound, and it still is.

71
00:06:27,221 --> 00:06:32,100
So that's another measure of how far off NASA estimates were.

72
00:06:32,101 --> 00:06:38,040
All of the goals of the shuttle program were dependent on its success as a business.

73
00:06:38,041 --> 00:06:41,480
Without a high number of launches, that success was impossible.

74
00:06:41,481 --> 00:06:45,420
The pressure on NASA leaders to increase flights was enormous.

75
00:06:45,421 --> 00:06:52,680
They were pushing the space shuttle system too hard, striving towards a launch rate of

76
00:06:52,681 --> 00:06:55,500
one every two weeks.

77
00:06:55,501 --> 00:07:03,300
Really we know now an impossible goal, stressing the system way beyond its limits.

78
00:07:03,301 --> 00:07:08,120
Trying to achieve an enormously ambitious goal with too little money and in too little time

79
00:07:08,121 --> 00:07:11,380
was all something the world had seen before.

80
00:07:11,381 --> 00:07:16,640
Unfortunately, few people at NASA were old enough to remember the story of another aircraft

81
00:07:16,641 --> 00:07:21,700
that struggled to fly almost 60 years before.

82
00:07:21,701 --> 00:07:30,500
In 1924, the British government set out to create a machine that had much in common with

83
00:07:30,501 --> 00:07:32,200
the space shuttle.

84
00:07:32,201 --> 00:07:37,640
The machine would be a 700-foot long dirigible that could carry passengers and tons of cargo

85
00:07:37,641 --> 00:07:40,880
to all parts of the British Empire.

86
00:07:40,881 --> 00:07:46,800
The spectacular new dirigible would be called the R101.

87
00:07:46,801 --> 00:07:52,200
Like NASA's space shuttle, the R101 was born out of great national aspirations.

88
00:07:52,201 --> 00:07:57,520
Like the shuttle, the R101 was plagued from the first with being behind schedule.

89
00:07:57,521 --> 00:08:01,940
And like the shuttle, the great dirigible was rushed forward without acknowledging many

90
00:08:01,941 --> 00:08:08,034
signs of danger. The R101 was the largest airship ever built to that time.

91
00:08:08,035 --> 00:08:08,940
Beneath the

92
00:08:09,700 --> 00:08:12,803
skin of the ship, a steel frame held 16 bags.

93
00:08:12,804 --> 00:08:15,700
Each one weighed half a ton and was filled

94
00:08:15,701 --> 00:08:22,233
with highly flammable hydrogen gas. Five enormous diesel engines supplied the power.

95
00:08:22,234 --> 00:08:22,934
But in

96
00:08:24,380 --> 00:08:29,940
creating extra margins of strength and power, the designers had built in its great weakness.

97
00:08:29,941 --> 00:08:34,220
Initial tests revealed the R101 was 23 tons too heavy.

98
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:40,280
This was a ship running on the ragged edge even under ideal circumstances.

99
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:49,520
A calm summer day had so little spare lift that motoring around the crew had to dump fuel to stay in the air.

100
00:08:51,920 --> 00:08:55,700
Lord Christopher Birdward Thompson, the Secretary of State for Air,

101
00:08:55,900 --> 00:08:59,500
was obsessed with demonstrating Britain's great dirigible to the world.

102
00:08:59,940 --> 00:09:06,360
In the summer of 1930, he brashly ordered the R-101 to attempt a maiden voyage to India.

103
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:11,300
Lord Thompson had decided this project had gone on long enough.

104
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:18,080
It needed to get into the air, even though the people closest to the project said,

105
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:21,520
this airship is positively dangerous.

106
00:09:21,521 --> 00:09:30,160
To show his confidence, Thompson was a passenger when the giant airship took off on the stormy night of October 4th, 1930.

107
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:37,240
By 2 a.m., the dirigible had made it to Beauvais, France, but was rapidly losing altitude.

108
00:09:37,980 --> 00:09:43,340
It barely cleared the roof of the town's Gothic cathedral and soon crashed into nearby trees.

109
00:09:43,341 --> 00:09:52,660
The explosion swept through the R101, killing 48 men, including Lord Thompson.

110
00:09:53,560 --> 00:09:59,500
All who died were victims of Thompson's impatience and unwillingness to place safety before schedule.

111
00:10:05,180 --> 00:10:10,560
56 years later, it was also schedule that continued to torment the space shuttle program.

112
00:10:10,561 --> 00:10:20,480
And on January 27, 1986, what was to be NASA's next launch had been delayed by three days of weather and technical holds.

113
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:23,500
The press began to criticize the delays.

114
00:10:24,580 --> 00:10:28,680
NASA fails to get it up one more time. It's a typical reaction.

115
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:37,300
People did not understand that, in fact, the reason they didn't go when they were supposed to was usually because there was a safety issue.

116
00:10:37,301 --> 00:10:43,540
They were concerned about launching, but delays got interpreted as failure.

117
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:51,480
The shuttle Challenger would be making this flight, and it was intended to be a public relations showpiece.

118
00:10:52,060 --> 00:10:56,960
In addition to plans for several experiments and delivery of a satellite and telescope into orbit,

119
00:10:57,640 --> 00:11:02,720
the shuttle carried a special passenger, a high school history teacher named Krista McAuliffe.

120
00:11:03,280 --> 00:11:05,940
Well, I am so excited to be here.

121
00:11:05,941 --> 00:11:13,980
McAuliffe was to be the third amateur astronaut to fly in the shuttle and would report the wonders of space to her students watching and listening below.

122
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:24,920
This latest liftoff date was also timed to allow President Ronald Reagan to mention its successful launch that night in his State of the Union speech.

123
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:32,380
NASA needed a perfect flight from Challenger, and it needed no more delays.

124
00:11:32,381 --> 00:11:35,120
But now, yet another problem had developed.

125
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:37,400
Engineers were worried about the weather.

126
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:47,360
As early as 1981, shuttle flights had shown that the safety of critical O-rings in the solid rocket boosters was jeopardized by cold weather.

127
00:11:48,540 --> 00:11:54,340
O-rings were round rubber gaskets resting in joints between the three sections of each solid fuel booster.

128
00:11:55,460 --> 00:12:01,420
Their purpose was to keep the hot gases of the rocket's combustion from escaping from between these sections.

129
00:12:01,421 --> 00:12:11,800
The thin rubber circles of the O-rings were all that stood between the booster's 5,000 degree heat and a half million gallons of incredibly explosive liquid fuel.

130
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:20,940
Engineers had come to the conclusion that these O-rings would become brittle at a temperature of 53 degrees.

131
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:28,000
Now on the night of January 27th, Challenger sat poised for launch the following morning.

132
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:32,860
Weather reports called for a liftoff temperature of 26 degrees.

133
00:12:34,380 --> 00:12:38,040
The Soviets' reusable space shuttle was the Boran.

134
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:42,280
It went into space one time in 1988 with no crew on board.

135
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:45,060
Inviting Disaster will continue on Modern Marvels.

136
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:49,760
You're watching Inviting Disaster on Modern Marvels.

137
00:12:51,540 --> 00:12:57,620
A skeptic is one who knows the system and yet knows what doesn't fit in.

138
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:06,940
And that's the kind of attitude, that's the kind of people that we want right in the middle of our complex high-power systems.

139
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:10,480
People who will raise informed, tough questions.

140
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:19,860
By the mid-1980s, NASA was having big trouble keeping the shuttle program on schedule.

141
00:13:20,380 --> 00:13:25,060
But when they did manage to get the orbiters into space, they had proven to be a success.

142
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,280
Research was being conducted, satellites were being launched,

143
00:13:31,700 --> 00:13:34,460
astronauts were becoming more adept at working in space.

144
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:37,540
In spite of their growing frustrations over the delays,

145
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:42,420
NASA was also growing ever more confident in the marvelous machine they had built.

146
00:13:42,940 --> 00:13:44,180
Perhaps too confident.

147
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:50,740
To build such a machine was a stunning accomplishment,

148
00:13:50,741 --> 00:13:55,760
but in its very technological prowess lay its greatest potential for disaster.

149
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:02,760
What we know about complex technical systems, like the shuttle system,

150
00:14:03,140 --> 00:14:08,980
is that the more complex they are, the more they are likely to have flaws.

151
00:14:09,740 --> 00:14:12,600
And we know the same thing about complex organizations.

152
00:14:12,601 --> 00:14:21,280
The bigger, the more difficult it is to retain a kind of a culture that's resistant to error.

153
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,420
January 27, 1986.

154
00:14:25,421 --> 00:14:31,180
After three days of delays, the Challenger waits to become the 25th shuttle mission.

155
00:14:33,260 --> 00:14:37,100
But now, on the eve of one of the most important launches in its history,

156
00:14:37,840 --> 00:14:43,320
the NASA organization had to deal with engineers who were insisting that the Challenger should not go up.

157
00:14:44,660 --> 00:14:47,160
The weather forecast for Florida called for cold.

158
00:14:47,980 --> 00:14:53,020
1,800 miles away in Utah, that forecast worried engineers at Morton Thiokol.

159
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:55,600
the builders of the solid fuel boosters.

160
00:14:56,280 --> 00:15:00,460
They were worried that the cold could cause O-ring seals in the boosters to fail.

161
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:06,480
On the night before the Challenger launch, this issue came to a head.

162
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:12,060
Thiokol asked for a meeting over a telephone line with NASA,

163
00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:18,240
and the solid rocket manager told NASA that he could not sign the papers for launch.

164
00:15:18,241 --> 00:15:25,380
NASA management was concerned about setting a precedent of 53 degrees as a limit for launch.

165
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:29,320
They wanted no more obstacles to getting this big bird up in the air.

166
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:32,460
It became very heated.

167
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:37,660
Viacol engineers were challenged on almost every engineering point.

168
00:15:38,700 --> 00:15:40,980
Engineers were arguing that they shouldn't go.

169
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:44,680
Manager was saying, well, where's your data?

170
00:15:44,681 --> 00:15:47,660
And they didn't have real data.

171
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:51,640
They had intuition and hunch, which didn't work.

172
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:53,680
It was not listened to.

173
00:15:54,260 --> 00:15:58,400
The burden of proof, then, is on those who would want to stop the flight,

174
00:15:58,720 --> 00:16:01,140
because it's a bad thing for you.

175
00:16:01,220 --> 00:16:06,760
It's a bad thing for your mission, for your reputation, for NASA to have to delay again.

176
00:16:06,860 --> 00:16:08,160
There was a lot of that going on.

177
00:16:09,100 --> 00:16:10,920
Thiokol engineers were intimidated.

178
00:16:11,420 --> 00:16:12,660
One NASA manager shouted,

179
00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:15,880
The next thing you know, I won't be able to launch until next April.

180
00:16:15,881 --> 00:16:29,881
At one point, NASA asked a manager at Thiokol to take off his engineering hat and to put on his management hat, as if that would make the decision more clear, the risk

181
00:16:31,188 --> 00:16:35,680
This meeting was very difficult. more acceptable.

182
00:16:36,560 --> 00:16:42,040
By the end of the meeting, pressure from NASA managers to keep the launch on schedule proved too much.

183
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:46,640
After discussing it amongst themselves, Viacol officials came to a decision.

184
00:16:47,540 --> 00:16:51,060
They went back on the teleconference line and said,

185
00:16:51,540 --> 00:16:57,500
we've reconsidered and we've decided to recommend launch.

186
00:16:58,420 --> 00:17:02,800
There was a degree of confidence in management that we knew what we were doing,

187
00:17:02,801 --> 00:17:07,520
and when they didn't hear the screaming and hollering from someone down below

188
00:17:07,521 --> 00:17:10,740
that we absolutely should not launch, management said to themselves,

189
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:14,460
well, we believe that we have a safe system, and they proceeded on.

190
00:17:16,720 --> 00:17:18,900
January 28, 1986.

191
00:17:19,440 --> 00:17:23,200
A cold dawn revealed a NASA launch pad covered with ice.

192
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:26,500
With hindsight, it seems impossible to believe

193
00:17:26,501 --> 00:17:29,560
that a decision to launch in these conditions could have been made.

194
00:17:29,561 --> 00:17:33,160
But it had been decided.

195
00:17:33,840 --> 00:17:37,020
Bureaucratic goals would take precedence over caution and safety.

196
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:42,900
The Challenger would lift off as planned.

197
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,700
Engineers closest to the data had been talked out of their objections.

198
00:17:50,260 --> 00:17:55,320
They had been required to prove their worst fears were true, rather than NASA proving they were not.

199
00:17:55,321 --> 00:18:03,100
But NASA had conditioned themselves to rationalize dangerous problems for the organizational goals of the program.

200
00:18:04,320 --> 00:18:07,880
Very dangerous. NASA got on a slippery slope.

201
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:16,500
One step at a time, they went from expecting there to be no damage to having some and then accepting more and more.

202
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:20,820
So on the eve of the Challenger launch, they made the decision to go,

203
00:18:20,821 --> 00:18:26,900
and it was just one more step in a procedure that had started quite a long time before that.

204
00:18:28,840 --> 00:18:33,660
NASA was gambling that the shuttle had never failed and wouldn't fail this time.

205
00:18:34,540 --> 00:18:37,120
Seven lives now hung on that gamble.

206
00:18:37,121 --> 00:18:41,600
Ten, nine, eight, seven, six.

207
00:18:42,140 --> 00:18:43,740
We have main engine start.

208
00:18:44,180 --> 00:18:47,080
Four, three, two, one.

209
00:18:47,900 --> 00:18:48,960
And liftoff.

210
00:18:50,820 --> 00:18:57,720
You're watching Inviting Disaster on Modern Marvels.

211
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:12,211
The disasters that I write about and that continue to unfold attract enormous media attention for a few days, for a few weeks, and in a few cases, continued attention

212
00:19:12,212 --> 00:19:13,560
for a few months.

213
00:19:13,561 --> 00:19:21,740
But in all those cases, the new cycle moves on before the full set of lessons emerge.

214
00:19:23,120 --> 00:19:28,060
T-minus 10, 9, 8, 7, 6.

215
00:19:28,580 --> 00:19:30,180
We have main engine start.

216
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:33,560
4, 3, 2, 1.

217
00:19:34,380 --> 00:19:35,400
And liftoff.

218
00:19:36,180 --> 00:19:40,600
Liftoff of the 25th base of the mission and it is clear in the town.

219
00:19:40,601 --> 00:19:47,940
If all disasters are preceded by hints, what was about to happen to Challenger had been

220
00:19:47,941 --> 00:19:56,840
preceded by voices pleading to postpone the mission.

221
00:19:56,841 --> 00:20:01,920
Demanding proof instead of listening to caution, NASA had gambled that the frigid O-rings

222
00:20:01,921 --> 00:20:04,980
in the solid booster would hold.

223
00:20:04,981 --> 00:20:07,980
It's getting throttling down now at 94%.

224
00:20:07,981 --> 00:20:11,920
58 seconds after liftoff, NASA lost the bet.

225
00:20:13,040 --> 00:20:19,660
A small flame emerged from the right solid rocket booster in one of the joints supposedly protected by its O-ring.

226
00:20:20,460 --> 00:20:25,900
Deflected by the force of air rushing past, the flame was turned against the side of the main fuel tank.

227
00:20:26,700 --> 00:20:28,940
Seconds later, Challenger disappeared.

228
00:20:34,980 --> 00:20:42,880
As families of the astronauts looked on with first confusion, then horror, the solid boosters flew off in wild contrails.

229
00:20:43,860 --> 00:20:49,300
NASA managers could only watch in stunned silence as the crew compartment tumbled through the smoky clouds.

230
00:20:52,740 --> 00:20:53,640
Flight photo.

231
00:20:53,920 --> 00:20:54,620
Go ahead.

232
00:20:54,621 --> 00:20:55,820
RSO reports vehicle exploded.

233
00:20:56,360 --> 00:20:59,460
The compartment would take over two minutes to fall into the sea.

234
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:00,700
Copy.

235
00:21:00,701 --> 00:21:11,620
Any astronaut going into space on board the space shuttle is sitting on hundreds of tons

236
00:21:11,621 --> 00:21:16,095
of explosive material. It is writing a controlled explosion into space.

237
00:21:16,096 --> 00:21:17,860
These people should not have

238
00:21:17,861 --> 00:21:23,320
died. There were many opportunities, clear warnings the night before not to launch.

239
00:21:23,321 --> 00:21:32,560
We thought we'd solved all the problems and that we were really operating to the best capability of the system.

240
00:21:33,100 --> 00:21:37,260
What we didn't realize, it's the little things that come up and bite you, O-rings.

241
00:21:38,300 --> 00:21:41,240
The nation lowered its head in shock and grief.

242
00:21:42,220 --> 00:21:45,520
They had been led to believe that flying the shuttle was routine.

243
00:21:46,060 --> 00:21:47,800
Even a high school teacher could go up.

244
00:21:48,280 --> 00:21:51,140
Now that teacher and her six companions were dead.

245
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:53,620
The public wanted to know why.

246
00:21:57,560 --> 00:22:01,740
A presidential commission headed by former Secretary of State William Rogers

247
00:22:01,741 --> 00:22:04,000
set out to find what had happened.

248
00:22:05,040 --> 00:22:08,440
As the inquiry began, NASA was not always forthcoming

249
00:22:08,441 --> 00:22:10,960
about what went into the decision to launch Challenger.

250
00:22:11,700 --> 00:22:13,420
In the first days after the disaster,

251
00:22:13,421 --> 00:22:16,740
NASA officials downplayed the significance of the O-rings

252
00:22:16,741 --> 00:22:18,600
and suggested other causes.

253
00:22:19,540 --> 00:22:29,240
When questioned by the Commission, NASA made no mention of Morton Thiokol's initial refusal to sign the order for the launch because of worries about the O-rings.

254
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:34,600
We are particularly interested in the weather conditions, how they may have affected the launch.

255
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:37,880
But little by little, the facts of Challenger came out.

256
00:22:38,360 --> 00:22:45,260
After months of analysis, the Rogers Commission confirmed that technically the disaster was caused by the failure of the solid rocket O-rings.

257
00:22:45,261 --> 00:22:52,260
But the commission concluded the loss of Challenger was more about a flawed decision-making process than about rubber gaskets.

258
00:22:52,261 --> 00:22:59,260
There was a lot of media attention about what were the causes of the Challenger disaster.

259
00:22:59,261 --> 00:23:04,260
What they thought, however, was that there was misconduct.

260
00:23:04,261 --> 00:23:07,260
What happened was actually much more frightening than that.

261
00:23:07,261 --> 00:23:11,260
There was no intentional misconduct.

262
00:23:11,261 --> 00:23:18,260
They did it exactly by the book, and they believed that they were correct and that it would be safe to fly.

263
00:23:18,261 --> 00:23:22,616
The flaw was not bad or incompetent or even evil people.

264
00:23:22,617 --> 00:23:25,260
The flaw was in the system itself.

265
00:23:25,261 --> 00:23:30,260
In an effort to achieve the same mission with less and less money,

266
00:23:30,261 --> 00:23:36,260
NASA had gone from accepting no damage to tolerating a larger and larger margin for error.

267
00:23:36,261 --> 00:23:36,961
error.

268
00:23:36,962 --> 00:23:45,760
The doctrine was to fly the shuttle, to fly it as frequently as possible, so as

269
00:23:45,761 --> 00:23:52,320
to make good on NASA's claim of what they were going to be able to do. In a world of

270
00:23:52,321 --> 00:23:57,380
higher expectations and lower and lower budgets, management decisions were motivated less and

271
00:23:57,381 --> 00:24:04,160
less by the opinions of engineers. And they lost touch with their own engineers

272
00:24:04,161 --> 00:24:06,400
and engineering knowledge.

273
00:24:06,401 --> 00:24:11,240
That, in fact, is what led them to start taking average citizens along for a ride.

274
00:24:11,241 --> 00:24:14,780
They didn't think it was risky.

275
00:24:14,781 --> 00:24:19,580
Out of the Commission report, specific changes were made to the shuttle program.

276
00:24:19,581 --> 00:24:24,080
Lives would no longer be risked to launch satellites that could more efficiently be put up with

277
00:24:24,081 --> 00:24:26,780
expendable rockets.

278
00:24:26,781 --> 00:24:31,780
In addition, NASA would abandon the concept of taking amateurs aboard flights.

279
00:24:31,781 --> 00:24:35,400
Krista McAuliffe would be the first and last teacher to ride the shuttle.

280
00:24:47,520 --> 00:24:52,580
Over two and a half years after the investigation of Challenger, the shuttle was ready to fly again.

281
00:24:53,300 --> 00:24:58,580
Changes had been made at NASA, but as years went by, it was clear that many of the problems remained.

282
00:24:58,581 --> 00:25:04,400
After Challenger we had a change in culture in that they thought if they threw more manpower

283
00:25:04,401 --> 00:25:06,754
at a particular problem you could solve it.

284
00:25:06,755 --> 00:25:09,820
What started happening is we got into the same mode that

285
00:25:09,821 --> 00:25:16,080
we were in prior to Challenger in that we got overconfident and not doing our homework about

286
00:25:16,081 --> 00:25:20,393
little things then it's the little things that always come up and get you.

287
00:25:20,394 --> 00:25:21,500
As the late 80s and

288
00:25:21,501 --> 00:25:25,289
90s went by NASA sent 87 more shuttles into orbit.

289
00:25:25,290 --> 00:25:28,320
The men and women who rode in them again

290
00:25:28,321 --> 00:25:30,300
dazzled the world with their accomplishments.

291
00:25:31,580 --> 00:25:35,640
With the shuttle's help, the International Space Station grew into a usable laboratory.

292
00:25:37,260 --> 00:25:41,500
In 1993, the crew of Endeavour repaired the damaged Hubble telescope,

293
00:25:42,320 --> 00:25:45,520
and always, research aboard the orbiter continued.

294
00:25:46,080 --> 00:25:52,120
But with all its successes, one part of the shuttle remained an accident waiting to happen.

295
00:25:53,460 --> 00:25:58,120
Among the thousands of components of technology that allow the shuttle to succeed,

296
00:25:58,320 --> 00:26:01,020
None is more important than the heat shield.

297
00:26:02,140 --> 00:26:05,240
Made up of tiles and carbon-carbon fiber panels,

298
00:26:05,960 --> 00:26:10,620
the shield is all that stands between the crew and the deadly heat of re-entry.

299
00:26:11,520 --> 00:26:16,100
The good news is that the ceramic tiles and panels can absorb enormous heat.

300
00:26:16,780 --> 00:26:19,000
The bad news is that they are very fragile.

301
00:26:20,900 --> 00:26:27,440
Debris hit and damaged tiles and carbon panels over 2,000 times in the first 33 flights of the shuttle.

302
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:35,180
In 1990, NASA asked a risk analysis team to analyze the life-protecting heat shield.

303
00:26:35,181 --> 00:26:36,755
So what did we find?

304
00:26:36,756 --> 00:26:46,440
Well, we found at the time that about 10% of the overall probability of an accident could be attributed to the heat shield.

305
00:26:46,680 --> 00:26:49,240
So that was a relatively high contribution.

306
00:26:49,740 --> 00:26:57,100
We also found that a large proportion of the risk was attributable to a relatively small proportion of the tiles.

307
00:26:58,320 --> 00:27:07,214
The report noted that tiles covering the wheel wells, and particularly the tiles and carbon-carbon panels along the leading edges of the wings, were the most

308
00:27:07,215 --> 00:27:08,460
potentially dangerous.

309
00:27:09,360 --> 00:27:19,486
If the tiles or carbon panels in these sensitive areas were lost or even severely cracked, heat could quickly penetrate the orbiter body and destroy critical control

310
00:27:19,487 --> 00:27:20,187
functions.

311
00:27:20,880 --> 00:27:27,280
The fragile nature of the heat shield meant that any blow by a foreign object during the mission could be disastrous.

312
00:27:27,281 --> 00:27:33,280
NASA had always been concerned about damage from asteroids or space debris while in orbit.

313
00:27:33,281 --> 00:27:38,280
But the tile research brought another threat which NASA would not acknowledge.

314
00:27:38,281 --> 00:27:46,280
The orbiter is attached to the main fuel tank, which holds a half million gallons of liquid hydrogen and oxygen.

315
00:27:46,281 --> 00:27:50,280
To keep this fuel at a temperature of over 400 degrees below zero,

316
00:27:50,281 --> 00:27:55,280
the huge tank is covered in six inches of foam insulation.

317
00:27:55,281 --> 00:28:00,680
By liftoff, the foam on the tank becomes frozen and hard as rock.

318
00:28:00,681 --> 00:28:06,020
The shuttle program is cautioned about the danger of this foam hitting the orbiter.

319
00:28:06,021 --> 00:28:11,340
NASA chooses to do nothing about the potential problem.

320
00:28:11,341 --> 00:28:15,520
It's this overconfidence that you can see happening in, oh well, if we miss it, the

321
00:28:15,521 --> 00:28:18,640
shuttle is very robust, it'll handle the situation.

322
00:28:18,641 --> 00:28:24,380
And that's also what the flight controllers were thinking about the tiles whenever the

323
00:28:24,381 --> 00:28:25,572
debris struck the tiles.

324
00:28:25,573 --> 00:28:28,700
The analysis they had done said that there's not a problem with

325
00:28:28,701 --> 00:28:32,100
this, so press on.

326
00:28:32,101 --> 00:28:36,620
For almost ten years after the report on the tiles, the space shuttle program did press

327
00:28:36,621 --> 00:28:41,992
on. In January 2003, NASA prepared to launch its next shuttle mission.

328
00:28:41,993 --> 00:28:43,220
The orbiter will

329
00:28:43,221 --> 00:28:48,710
be the same Columbia that had made the very first shuttle trip.

330
00:28:48,711 --> 00:28:50,540
The crew of this trip

331
00:28:50,541 --> 00:28:55,580
will again be a mix of dedicated and very talented pilots and scientists. Typically,

332
00:28:55,581 --> 00:29:00,940
it is a diverse group that includes an African American, an Israeli, an Indian, those who

333
00:29:00,941 --> 00:29:06,380
had flown on other shuttle missions, and two who had never been in space before.

334
00:29:06,381 --> 00:29:11,220
The Columbia will carry them into orbit where they will conduct 14 days of experiments ranging

335
00:29:11,221 --> 00:29:16,394
from analyzing dust storms in the Middle East to studying prostate cancer cells.

336
00:29:16,395 --> 00:29:17,300
The astronauts

337
00:29:17,301 --> 00:29:24,300
Students will also conduct experiments suggested by high school students from around the world.

338
00:29:26,300 --> 00:29:33,300
On the morning of January 16, 2003, a NASA space shuttle sits poised to go into space for the 113th time.

339
00:29:34,300 --> 00:29:39,300
Like dozens of their colleagues before them, the Columbia astronauts board their spacecraft

340
00:29:40,300 --> 00:29:45,300
and prepare to take the most exciting ride in the world.

341
00:29:47,300 --> 00:29:55,300
We have booster ignition and liftoff of space shuttle Columbia with a multitude of national

342
00:29:55,301 --> 00:29:58,300
and international space research experiments.

343
00:29:58,301 --> 00:30:04,300
Once again, the world would thrill at watching this amazing machine slip the bonds of gravity

344
00:30:04,301 --> 00:30:09,300
and climb into space. And once again, it appeared to be a perfect launch.

345
00:30:09,301 --> 00:30:11,300
Columbia now rolling on to the proper end.

346
00:30:11,301 --> 00:30:15,300
But no one had noticed that as the monstrous rocket roared off the pad,

347
00:30:15,301 --> 00:30:20,740
A piece of frozen insulation had broken loose from the fuel tank and smashed into the left wing.

348
00:30:21,740 --> 00:30:23,860
The piece of foam was no bigger than a suitcase.

349
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,140
Houston now controlling the flight of Columbia.

350
00:30:28,440 --> 00:30:33,020
But its effects would take seven lives and threaten the entire shuttle program.

351
00:30:33,021 --> 00:30:34,340
And you're on Ramon.

352
00:30:35,420 --> 00:30:40,640
For the first four flights, the shuttle Columbia featured ejection seats for the two pilots.

353
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:43,740
The larger crew made the ejection plan impractical.

354
00:30:44,300 --> 00:30:46,540
Inviting Disaster will continue on Modern Marvels.

355
00:30:48,340 --> 00:30:51,680
You're watching Inviting Disaster on Modern Marvels.

356
00:30:52,700 --> 00:30:55,380
I have a lot of respect for NASA and what they've done.

357
00:30:55,820 --> 00:31:02,360
I've attended the space shuttle launches and thrilled to it like any other person within hearing range.

358
00:31:03,320 --> 00:31:08,160
But I think NASA has to confront directly the criticism from well-motivated people

359
00:31:08,161 --> 00:31:11,740
that the space shuttle has become a hood ornament,

360
00:31:11,741 --> 00:31:14,960
a symbol, a luxury.

361
00:31:15,920 --> 00:31:18,740
You can see the Earth kind of at the top of the screen there.

362
00:31:19,200 --> 00:31:21,520
We're looking to the left side of the orbiter

363
00:31:21,521 --> 00:31:25,380
when the radiator on the left payload bay door comes up.

364
00:31:26,240 --> 00:31:28,240
Columbia has reached orbit successfully,

365
00:31:28,460 --> 00:31:30,640
and the crew is well into their routine

366
00:31:30,641 --> 00:31:34,460
when engineers at NASA realize that something potentially dangerous

367
00:31:34,461 --> 00:31:35,980
has happened on liftoff.

368
00:31:37,580 --> 00:31:39,540
They have finally seen the video

369
00:31:39,541 --> 00:31:43,900
that reveals the suitcase-sized hunk of foam hitting the left wing of the orbiter.

370
00:31:45,520 --> 00:31:48,440
The question is, how bad is the damage?

371
00:31:50,460 --> 00:31:54,220
Launch video suggests any damage would have been under the wing,

372
00:31:54,620 --> 00:31:59,700
so the Columbia's crew cannot check the tiles themselves without an unplanned spacewalk.

373
00:32:00,360 --> 00:32:03,580
The shuttle has no power to be able to move into a new orbit

374
00:32:03,581 --> 00:32:07,880
so that astronauts riding in the space station could inspect the wing for damage.

375
00:32:09,540 --> 00:32:14,100
Engineers at the Johnson Space Center in Houston can only guess at the potential damage

376
00:32:14,101 --> 00:32:18,260
by analyzing the puff of dust made when the foam hit the shuttle.

377
00:32:19,300 --> 00:32:22,980
They urgently request that satellites be ordered to take photos of the wing.

378
00:32:23,740 --> 00:32:28,320
If terminal damage is confirmed, NASA leaders would face a choice of doing nothing

379
00:32:28,321 --> 00:32:30,980
