380
00:32:32,020 --> 00:32:37,140
But the choice becomes unnecessary when the head of the mission management team, Linda Hamm,

381
00:32:37,141 --> 00:32:40,700
decides to cancel the engineer's request for photos.

382
00:32:44,160 --> 00:32:48,160
And it makes me wonder whether an attitude might have set in,

383
00:32:48,240 --> 00:32:49,600
well, why do we need to photograph?

384
00:32:50,620 --> 00:32:52,840
Because what will we find out?

385
00:32:52,980 --> 00:32:56,160
What will we do if we see fatal damage?

386
00:32:56,280 --> 00:32:57,540
What are we going to tell the astronauts?

387
00:32:58,620 --> 00:33:01,680
It is now certain that at some time during liftoff,

388
00:33:01,681 --> 00:33:04,340
the leading edge of the left wing was damaged.

389
00:33:04,341 --> 00:33:07,180
additional carbon fiber fell off in space.

390
00:33:08,180 --> 00:33:11,880
This leading edge is protected by a carbon-carbon fiber

391
00:33:11,881 --> 00:33:14,660
that, like the tiles themselves, resists heat.

392
00:33:15,820 --> 00:33:19,900
Without it, the inner structure of the orbiter is completely vulnerable.

393
00:33:22,640 --> 00:33:25,960
Knowing nothing about the possible threat to their spacecraft,

394
00:33:26,580 --> 00:33:29,220
the crew of the Columbia allow themselves the fun

395
00:33:29,221 --> 00:33:32,440
of being part of one of humankind's greatest adventures.

396
00:33:32,441 --> 00:33:39,520
Here comes the Superman entry of the astronauts floating around in the space.

397
00:33:40,280 --> 00:33:43,400
We have a best-tier 107 coming into the module.

398
00:33:43,980 --> 00:33:45,880
He's here to do some house cleaning duties.

399
00:33:46,420 --> 00:33:49,220
But life on a space shuttle is mostly about research.

400
00:33:50,360 --> 00:33:55,880
High school students in Syracuse, New York, wanted to know if ants move faster in space than on Earth.

401
00:33:56,300 --> 00:33:57,620
Columbia proves that they do.

402
00:33:57,621 --> 00:34:05,940
A combustion experiment studying possible sources for low-power engines manages to produce the weakest flame to ever continue burning.

403
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:10,400
Dr. Laurel Clark looks at cell cultures for cancer research.

404
00:34:11,420 --> 00:34:14,840
And Mike Anderson analyzes respiration and heart rate in space.

405
00:34:15,560 --> 00:34:20,680
It has been a successful flight, but after 16 days, the time arrives to come down.

406
00:34:20,681 --> 00:34:28,580
Saturday, February 1st, 2003, 8.15 a.m. Eastern Time.

407
00:34:29,060 --> 00:34:33,620
Somewhere over the Indian Ocean, Commander Husband is given the order to commence re-entry.

408
00:34:34,940 --> 00:34:37,444
We're checking out. We've got the flight controller power on.

409
00:34:37,445 --> 00:34:39,660
We're working through the rest of you as well. Thanks.

410
00:34:40,980 --> 00:34:45,220
Rockets fire to slow the shuttle's speed of 20 times the speed of sound.

411
00:34:45,900 --> 00:34:49,140
Computers reorient the aircraft to a nose-up position.

412
00:34:49,141 --> 00:34:56,620
As it crosses over Hawaii, Columbia begins to re-enter Earth's atmosphere in a descent from which there is no turning back.

413
00:34:57,320 --> 00:35:01,660
Few machines are put through the stress demanded of an orbiter returning to Earth.

414
00:35:04,700 --> 00:35:10,360
The friction of the growing number of molecules in the atmosphere rapidly increases heat on Columbia.

415
00:35:11,000 --> 00:35:16,760
The orbiter is moving from 100 degrees below zero to over 3,000 degrees in minutes.

416
00:35:16,761 --> 00:35:20,760
This is amazing. It's really getting really bright out there.

417
00:35:20,761 --> 00:35:27,760
Columbia's crew can watch as gases outside the windows turn from pink to red to a searing white.

418
00:35:27,761 --> 00:35:31,760
Yeah, you definitely don't want to be outside now.

419
00:35:31,761 --> 00:35:36,760
All of this is routine. Shuttles have gone through it 113 times.

420
00:35:36,761 --> 00:35:41,760
But somewhere over San Francisco, the reentry stops being routine.

421
00:35:41,761 --> 00:35:48,120
FYI, I've just lost four separate temperature transducers on the left side of the vehicle.

422
00:35:48,680 --> 00:35:50,180
Where is that instrumentation located?

423
00:35:51,800 --> 00:35:54,980
All four of them are located in the aft part of the left wing.

424
00:35:56,060 --> 00:35:59,680
Temperature sensors in the left wing's hydraulic systems have stopped working.

425
00:36:00,740 --> 00:36:04,680
Minutes later, the temperature in the wing's landing gear jumps up 60 degrees,

426
00:36:05,140 --> 00:36:08,840
and shortly after, all communication with the orbiter ends abruptly.

427
00:36:08,841 --> 00:36:13,180
flight controllers desperately try to get some response from the shuttle.

428
00:36:14,500 --> 00:36:17,060
Columbia, Houston, UHF, comm check.

429
00:36:20,140 --> 00:36:22,620
Columbia, Houston, UHF, comm check.

430
00:36:27,540 --> 00:36:33,800
Looking up at the morning sky, observers in Texas already know why the Columbia is not responding.

431
00:36:38,840 --> 00:36:46,480
Once again, America grieves at the loss of seven talented and dedicated people.

432
00:36:47,120 --> 00:36:52,880
And once again, authorities turn their attention to asking the sad question, why?

433
00:36:56,160 --> 00:37:00,340
In 2001, NASA considered taking Columbia out of service

434
00:37:00,341 --> 00:37:03,880
because it was the oldest shuttle and had the least cargo-lifting capacity.

