452
00:38:14,141 --> 00:38:17,140
It's rising, it's rising, it's rising terrible.

453
00:38:17,141 --> 00:38:21,140
It took only minutes for the Hindenburg to become a smoking mass of rubble.

454
00:38:21,141 --> 00:38:25,140
This is terrible. This is one of the worst catastrophes in the world.

455
00:38:25,141 --> 00:38:30,140
But in those minutes, decades of work and research came to a shuddering halt.

456
00:38:30,141 --> 00:38:37,180
Commercial flight in dirigibles, what had been an entire category of aviation, vanished with the Hindenburg.

457
00:38:38,020 --> 00:38:47,260
Explosion and fire shut down the notion of using lighter-than-air aircraft as mass transportation.

458
00:38:47,261 --> 00:38:50,520
Never again would people ride such airships.

459
00:38:50,521 --> 00:39:00,440
Time will tell whether the loss of two of the shuttle fleet will spell the end to this phase of manned spaceflight.

460
00:39:03,460 --> 00:39:04,900
March 2003.

461
00:39:06,880 --> 00:39:12,200
The loss of seven more NASA astronauts comes as a surprise and shock for some people.

462
00:39:12,201 --> 00:39:17,500
But for others, another tragedy in the shuttle program has been only a matter of time.

463
00:39:18,440 --> 00:39:20,400
Columbia definitely was not a surprise to me.

464
00:39:20,401 --> 00:39:24,040
I had been saying for a long time that this was going to happen.

465
00:39:26,500 --> 00:39:32,060
The woods and fields of East Texas are combed to find clues to what had destroyed Columbia.

466
00:39:33,080 --> 00:39:35,860
Welcome to our first Washington press briefing.

467
00:39:36,180 --> 00:39:38,720
I will turn you over to Admiral Harold Gaiman.

468
00:39:39,160 --> 00:39:39,860
Good afternoon.

469
00:39:40,320 --> 00:39:43,840
Obviously, the board is toward the end of its transition.

470
00:39:44,200 --> 00:39:47,120
The investigating board is headed by retired Admiral Harold Gaiman.

471
00:39:48,160 --> 00:39:49,180
His job is daunting.

472
00:39:49,900 --> 00:39:52,800
Tracking down the cause of Columbia's loss will be very difficult.

473
00:39:53,580 --> 00:39:55,860
It was clear that foam had hit the shuttle,

474
00:39:56,380 --> 00:39:59,400
but this was not conclusive proof that it caused the breakup.

475
00:40:02,200 --> 00:40:06,920
Experts differed on whether a small piece of foam could cause the destruction of Columbia.

476
00:40:09,800 --> 00:40:13,620
NASA officials in particular seem eager to discourage the theory

477
00:40:13,621 --> 00:40:17,780
that foam falling from the fuel tank could be the cause of such a massive failure.

478
00:40:18,440 --> 00:40:28,820
Right now, it just does not make sense to us that a piece of debris would be the root cause for the loss of Columbia and its crew.

479
00:40:29,940 --> 00:40:33,300
We shouldn't be so quick to rush to that kind of judgment.

480
00:40:33,900 --> 00:40:42,720
I think what we were seeing is a very common reaction among organizations following a disaster, which is to circle the wagons.

481
00:40:43,720 --> 00:40:48,020
The board decides that they will have to organize a test to get more evidence.

482
00:40:48,900 --> 00:40:53,140
They need to see if they can find the smoking gun to the Columbia disaster.

483
00:40:55,080 --> 00:41:01,560
Four months after the accident, a piece of foam is fired from an air-powered cannon at the leading edge of a shuttle wing.

484
00:41:02,520 --> 00:41:05,540
Three, two, one, zero.

485
00:41:07,060 --> 00:41:12,540
Even untrained eyes could see that the foam had cracked the carbon-carbon panels on the wing's edge.

486
00:41:12,720 --> 00:41:17,020
But some insisted that this level of damage could not be counted as proof.

487
00:41:17,021 --> 00:41:18,960
A second test was ordered.

488
00:41:20,080 --> 00:41:23,000
Three, two, one, zero.

489
00:41:25,780 --> 00:41:29,240
This time, there is no mistaking the effects of the foam.

490
00:41:30,220 --> 00:41:33,140
I believe that we have found the smoking gun.

491
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:37,840
I believe that we've established that the foam block fell off of the external tank.

492
00:41:38,480 --> 00:41:42,480
It was, in fact, the most probable cause of the Columbia accident.

493
00:41:42,720 --> 00:41:49,980
It was now clear that the foam had damaged the leading edge of Columbia's wing.

494
00:41:50,680 --> 00:41:54,660
This allowed the scalding gases of re-entry to burn through the shuttle's skin

495
00:41:54,661 --> 00:41:57,240
and penetrate the interior of the wing itself.

496
00:41:58,720 --> 00:42:01,700
There, the heat melted the aluminum structure of the wing.

497
00:42:02,760 --> 00:42:06,000
Aerodynamic forces then pulled the orbiter apart.

498
00:42:07,460 --> 00:42:12,000
But as with Challenger, the deeper roots of the tragedy were not found in physical breakdowns.

499
00:42:12,720 --> 00:42:19,640
The report of the investigating board stated unequivocally that NASA's organizational culture and structure

500
00:42:19,641 --> 00:42:22,800
had as much to do with the accident as foam insulation.

501
00:42:23,680 --> 00:42:26,860
The bad habits from the time of Challenger had returned.

502
00:42:29,320 --> 00:42:31,160
Overconfidence was one of those habits.

503
00:42:31,720 --> 00:42:38,120
The Columbia project manager said that they had been having tile problems all along,

504
00:42:38,260 --> 00:42:39,720
but they were comfortable with that.

505
00:42:40,300 --> 00:42:42,120
Comfortable is not what you want to be.

506
00:42:42,720 --> 00:42:48,860
The report noted that opinions dissenting from NASA's upper management were not tolerated.

507
00:42:48,861 --> 00:42:55,120
The result was that ideas and potentially useful criticism were being squelched.

508
00:42:55,121 --> 00:42:59,740
Even more damning, the report stated that unless serious changes are made, the stage

509
00:42:59,741 --> 00:43:03,940
is set for yet another NASA catastrophe.

510
00:43:03,941 --> 00:43:07,840
The investigation board supported a return to flight for the shuttle at the earliest

511
00:43:07,841 --> 00:43:14,840
but only if serious changes are made in NASA's safety standards and patterns of management.

512
00:43:14,841 --> 00:43:18,840
For some, the system and the machine are too deeply flawed.

513
00:43:18,841 --> 00:43:23,840
For the first time in its history, many people question whether the time has come to end the shuttle program

514
00:43:23,841 --> 00:43:28,840
and perhaps even abandon manned space exploration altogether.

515
00:43:28,841 --> 00:43:37,660
As the space shuttle has succeeded, it has symbolized, like nothing before it, our ability

516
00:43:37,661 --> 00:43:41,160
to conceive and create amazing machines.

517
00:43:41,161 --> 00:43:45,780
But the shuttle program has also displayed the unfortunate human tendency for impatience

518
00:43:45,781 --> 00:43:48,180
and bureaucratic inefficiency.

519
00:43:48,181 --> 00:43:54,900
The problems lie not with the machines, but with our own imperfect ability to manage them.

520
00:43:54,901 --> 00:43:57,000
We cannot go back to the farm.

521
00:43:57,001 --> 00:44:03,540
There are too many people on Earth with too many expectations of the life that we have

522
00:44:03,541 --> 00:44:09,720
built for ourselves, and technology is how we feed these people, how we keep them safe,

523
00:44:09,721 --> 00:44:11,520
how we medicate them.

524
00:44:11,521 --> 00:44:17,320
So I am not one of those people that say technology is the enemy.

525
00:44:17,321 --> 00:44:23,400
Instead I see technology as a tool that needs to be used in the right way.

526
00:44:23,401 --> 00:44:28,020
Will we continue to see a shuttle program plagued with dangerous problems and prone to

527
00:44:28,021 --> 00:44:30,320
unnecessary accidents?

528
00:44:30,321 --> 00:44:35,560
Or will we at last learn from our mistakes and demand that an amazing machine head for

529
00:44:35,561 --> 00:44:40,080
the heavens without inviting disaster?

530
00:44:40,081 --> 00:44:46,720
This book is powerful.

531
00:44:46,721 --> 00:44:49,540
Millions seek solace in its message of peace.

532
00:44:49,541 --> 00:44:51,640
Others use it to justify war.

533
00:44:51,641 --> 00:44:55,040
It's a rule book about how to live one's life.

534
00:44:55,041 --> 00:44:58,800
To understand how Muslims worldwide read it as the Word of God

535
00:44:58,801 --> 00:45:02,180
means decoding the past to learn the secrets of the Koran.

536
00:45:02,181 --> 00:45:04,300
Tonight at 9 on the History Channel.

